{"name":"Lucky Clover","hex":"#008400","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lucky clover","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A four-leaf clover, believed to bring good luck\nAny four-leaf variant of the normally three-leaf species of clover of genus Trifolium\nAny of certain normally four-leaf plants similar to clover, including\nOxalis tetraphylla (four-leaf sorrel)\nMarsilea quadrifolia (European water-clover)","Any four-leaf variant of the normally three-leaf species of clover of genus Trifolium"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lucky_clover"}],"entries":[{"title":"Four-leaf clover","blocks":[{"text":"The four-leaf clover is a rare mutation of the common three-leaf clover that has four leaflets instead of three. According to tradition, such clovers bring good luck, a belief that dates back to at least the 17th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg/330px-Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-leaf_clover","wikidata":"Q756314"},{"title":"Clover","blocks":[{"text":"Clovers, also called trefoils, are plants of the genus Trifolium. They are herbaceous plants growing up to 30 cm (12 in) tall. The leaves are usually trifoliate and the small flowers are reddish to white or yellow. Related genera with similar common names include Melilotus and Medicago (burclover)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/79_Trifolium_repens_L.jpg/330px-79_Trifolium_repens_L.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clover","wikidata":"Q101538"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg/500px-Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte